Do not compare missing values as if they were equivalent.

Show unavailable data clearly and avoid making unsupported claims about which vehicle is better.

## 21. PERFORMANCE AND ASSET OPTIMIZATION

The configurator must be optimized for realistic browser conditions.

Requirements:
- Lazy-load 3D components.
- Load only the active vehicle model.
- Optimize model geometry and texture sizes.
- Use appropriate compression when compatible.
- Avoid unnecessary rerenders.
- Handle slow connections.
- Provide a static image fallback.
- Dispose of WebGL resources appropriately.
- Test memory usage when switching vehicles.
- Keep the CRM fast even when the 3D viewer is not loaded.

Do not choose asset quality solely by the appearance of a desktop render. Test actual browser loading performance.

## 22. SECURITY REQUIREMENTS

Implement:
- Server-side input validation.
- Authorization checks for protected records.
- Supabase RLS policies.
- Secure environment-variable handling.
- Rate limiting on public endpoints.
- Appropriate webhook authentication.
- Safe error responses.
- Safe logging.
- Duplicate-request protection.
- Auditing of important business changes.
- Least-privilege access for integrations.
- Protection against unauthorized record access.
- Correct handling of customer communication preferences.

Never commit secrets to GitHub.

Never expose service-role keys, private API keys or privileged tokens to browser code.

Do not claim a security policy is effective merely because the UI hides a button.

Test authorization boundaries with different roles.

## 23. ERROR HANDLING AND OBSERVABILITY

Every major feature should have useful loading, empty, success and error states.

Examples:
- Vehicle asset cannot load.
- Unsupported customization option.
- Supabase request fails.
- Inquiry validation fails.
- Duplicate submission is detected.
- n8n endpoint is unavailable.
- HubSpot rejects a request.
- Resend fails to send a message.
- AI provider is unavailable.

Errors should be actionable and safe.

Do not expose internal stack traces, tokens, private data or credentials to users.

Record enough operational information to diagnose integration problems without storing unnecessary personal data.

